{
  "title": "Subcoracoid Impingement",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/shoulder-and-elbow/3040/subcoracoid-impingement",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 3 2021",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Subcoracoid impingement is the impingement of the subscapularis between the coracoid and lesser tuberosity which can lead to anterior shoulder pain and possibly tearing of the subscapularis.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made clinically with tenderness over the anterior coracoid which is made worse with shoulder flexion/internal rotation and supplemented with CT scan showing decreased coracohumeral interval.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is a course of conservative measures including NSAIDs, physical therapy and corticosteroid injections. Arthroscopic or open coracoplasty is indicated for patients with progressive symptoms having failed conservative measures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathoanatomy",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "position of maximal impingement is arm **adduction**,**flexion**, and **internal rotation**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/illustration.jpg",
                      "caption": "illustration"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "risk factors",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "patients with a **long or excessively lateral coracoid process**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "prior surgery that caused **posterior capsular tightening** and loss of internal rotation",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Associated conditions",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "combined subscapularis, supraspinatus, and infraspinatus tears",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Glenohumeral joint anatomy and biomechanics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Coracoid",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "muscle attachments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "coracobrachialis, pectoralis minor, and short head of the biceps attach to the coracoid",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "ligamentous attachments",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "coracohumeral ligament, coracoacromial ligament attach to coracoid",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "coracoclavicular ligament which is composed of the conoid and the trapezium",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Subscapularis tendon**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "inserts onto lesser tuberosity",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Presentation",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Symptoms",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "pain in anterior shoulder worsened by various degrees of flexion, adduction, and rotation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Physical exam",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "tenderness over anterior coracoid",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "position of maximal pain is 120-130° of arm flexion and internal rotation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "recommended views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "may show a decreased coracohumeral distance",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "obtained with the **arms crossed on chest** is helpful to make the diagnosis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "a coracohumeral distance of **< 6 mm** is considered abnormal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "normal is 8.7 mm in the adducted arm 6.7 mm in the flexed arm",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "indications",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "used to evaluate degree of rotator cuff pathology",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "findings",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "increased signal in subscapularis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/tendon.jpg",
                      "caption": "Axial T2-weighted MRI showing increased signal in subscapularis tendon"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "increased signal in lesser tuberosity",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/tuberosity.jpg",
                      "caption": "Axial T2-weighted image showing increased signal in lesser tuberosity"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "views",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "axial view also effective to look for a decreased coracohumeral distance",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/mri.jpg",
                      "caption": "MRI"
                    },
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/MRI%20of%20subcoracoid%20stenosis_moved.jpg",
                      "caption": ""
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Studies",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Diagnostic injection",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "local corticosteroid injections should eliminate symptoms and can be diagnostic",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Nonoperative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "rest, ice, activity modification, NSAIDS, corticosteroid injections",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "first line of treatment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "techniques",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "local corticosteroid injections can be diagnostic and therapeutic",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "PT focuses on stretching",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Operative",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "arthroscopic coracoplasty ± subscapularis repair",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/coracoplasty%20for%20subcoracoid%20impingement_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "symptoms refractory to conservative treatment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "subscapularis tearing secondary to impingement",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "resect posterolateral coracoid to create **7 mm clearance** between coracoid and subscapularis",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "if significant subscapularis tendon tear then repair",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [
                        {
                          "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/3040/images/scope%20pic.jpg",
                          "caption": "scope"
                        }
                      ],
                      "importance": "4",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "open coracoplasty",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "indications",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "symptoms refractory to conservative treatment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "subscapularis tearing secondary to impingement",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "technique",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "resect lateral aspect of coracoid process and **reattach the conjoined tendon** to the remaining coracoid",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
